## What I changed

src/format/mod.rs — split the context-budget warning into a pure core plus a thin global wrapper:

• `budget_warning_threshold(used, max) -> Option<u32> — pure ladder (95/90/80/60), max == 0` → None, below 60 → None.
• budget_warning_message(threshold) -> Option<String> — pure renderer, the four strings byte-for-byte unchanged.
• context_budget_warning_with(&AtomicU32, used, max) — escalation logic over **injected** state.
• context_budget_warning is now a one-line delegation with &LAST_WARNED_THRESHOLD; `reset_context_budget_warning untouched. dispatch.rs, commands_session.rs, prompt.rs` compile unchanged.

Tests: all nine test_context_budget_warning_* tests kept (same thresholds, same substring assertions) but now drive a local AtomicU32 — local state can't be raced, so the flake class is gone by construction, not by a wider lock. Added two pure-half tests (ladder table incl. over-budget clamp and max == 0; unknown-threshold → None) and exactly one #[serial] wiring test that asserts context_budget_warning really writes/reads LAST_WARNED_THRESHOLD and reset_* re-arms that state — otherwise the refactor could silently disconnect the real function and every local test would stay green. BUDGET_WARNING_TEST_LOCK stays, used only by that one test.

## Verification

• cargo build, cargo test → 4600 passed, 0 failed; cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings clean; cargo fmt -- --check clean.
• `cargo test context_budget_warning -- --test-threads=8` ×3 → 10/10 every run.
• CLAUDE.md's format/mod.rs bullet updated.

## One honest correction to the task's hypothesis

I checked for the racer and did not find one. reset_context_budget_warning is only called from dispatch.rs (×2) and `commands_session.rs, and context_budget_warning from prompt.rs:76` — all inside agent-driven handlers with no test reaching them (grep for handle_compact/dispatch_command( in test context returned nothing). So the mechanism that produced that specific CI failure is still unexplained; I'm not going to claim I fixed it. What I can say is the global-state coupling that *would* make such a race possible is now removed from the test path, which is the durable half of the task either way.
